1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,717
Martin Sloane had waited nine months to meet the
thing in the satellite image. He was

2
00:00:13,737 --> 00:00:19,306
a patient man. He had once waited four years in
Tbilisi for a single phone call.

3
00:00:19,876 --> 00:00:25,518
They met in June, in the private room of a club in
Georgetown that did not have a name

4
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:31,114
on its door, at a table by a fire that was lit even
though it was summer, because the

5
00:00:31,134 --> 00:00:36,842
club had been lighting it every evening since
nineteen fifty-three and saw no reason to

6
00:00:36,862 --> 00:00:37,171
stop.

7
00:00:37,862 --> 00:00:39,057
You know what I saw.

8
00:00:39,578 --> 00:00:45,103
A satellite passed over Nevada in November. I
assume someone at Langley was awake.

9
00:00:45,624 --> 00:00:51,118
Someone at Langley was very awake. Twelve heat
signatures in. One out. And a helicopter

10
00:00:51,138 --> 00:00:54,984
that's now, I'm told, a very popular
new resident of Sinaloa.

11
00:00:56,384 --> 00:01:00,585
I'm not going to ask you what it was. I'm not going
to ask you what else you have. I'm

12
00:01:00,605 --> 00:01:04,905
going to tell you what I want, and then I'm going to
tell you what I'll give you for it,

13
00:01:04,925 --> 00:01:06,475
and then you're going to decide.

14
00:01:06,995 --> 00:01:08,084
That's refreshing.

15
00:01:08,605 --> 00:01:10,974
I'm told you like people who don't
waste your time.

16
00:01:12,875 --> 00:01:17,868
What I want is simple. There are things the United
States government would like to be

17
00:01:17,888 --> 00:01:23,294
able to do, in the world, that it cannot do with its
own hands. Biological things. Defensive

18
00:01:23,314 --> 00:01:28,484
things, mostly. Detection. Countermeasures. Vaccines
that exist before the disease does.

19
00:01:28,504 --> 00:01:33,497
And, occasionally, other things. The kind of work
that, if a government did it, would

20
00:01:33,517 --> 00:01:38,511
require a signature from someone elected. The kind
of work that, if a private company

21
00:01:38,531 --> 00:01:42,227
did it, under contract, would require
only a signature from me.

22
00:01:42,746 --> 00:01:43,849
Black contracts.

23
00:01:44,367 --> 00:01:48,870
I prefer the term classified procurement. It
sounds so much less like a film.

24
00:01:50,391 --> 00:01:51,459
And in exchange?

25
00:01:51,978 --> 00:01:57,330
In exchange, the investigations stop. Not the Bureau —
the Bureau's already stopped, you've

26
00:01:57,350 --> 00:02:02,524
seen to that. I mean the others. The ones you haven't
met yet. The Department of Defense

27
00:02:02,544 --> 00:02:07,542
has a unit that watches for technological surprise.
So does the Department of Energy.

28
00:02:07,562 --> 00:02:12,559
So does a very small office at the National
Security Council whose director reads the

29
00:02:12,579 --> 00:02:18,167
same German patent reports that Julian Ashcombe does.
They're all going to find you eventually.

30
00:02:18,187 --> 00:02:23,538
I can make sure they find you as a contractor. A
classified one. And classified contractors

31
00:02:23,558 --> 00:02:25,900
are not investigated. They're protected.

32
00:02:27,919 --> 00:02:33,355
The Director was silent for a while. He looked at
the fire. The Cold-Read had already

33
00:02:33,375 --> 00:02:39,067
told him everything about Martin Sloane there was to
know: that he was telling the truth,

34
00:02:39,087 --> 00:02:44,780
that he was not telling all of it, and that he
intended, in the long run, to own Umbrella

35
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:50,363
the way he owned every asset he had ever run — as a
tool, to be used and then put away.

36
00:02:50,933 --> 00:02:55,818
That was fine. The Director intended exactly
the same thing in reverse.

37
00:02:57,508 --> 00:02:59,719
He thinks he is buying you.

38
00:03:00,241 --> 00:03:03,506
He is buying me. He just doesn't know
what he's paying with.

39
00:03:04,026 --> 00:03:06,097
And what is he paying with?

40
00:03:06,616 --> 00:03:12,701
Access. To every secret his government has about
biology. Every programme. Every laboratory.

41
00:03:12,721 --> 00:03:18,739
Every weakness. He's going to open the door to the
house and invite me to fix the plumbing.

42
00:03:20,639 --> 00:03:26,182
I'll need the first contract to be real, Mister
Sloane. Not a test. Something that matters.

43
00:03:26,701 --> 00:03:27,550
It will be.

44
00:03:28,072 --> 00:03:32,187
And I'll need you to understand that I don't
do anything I can't finish.

45
00:03:32,707 --> 00:03:36,591
I watched the satellite, Doctor Wesker. I
understand that very well.

46
00:03:38,611 --> 00:03:44,917
They shook hands over the fire. Sloane's grip was dry
and firm and did not hold on a moment

47
00:03:44,937 --> 00:03:50,408
too long, and the Director, for once, did not
hold on a moment too long either.

48
00:03:50,979 --> 00:03:57,588
It was, a historian would write decades later — in a
world that had stopped allowing historians

49
00:03:57,608 --> 00:04:03,798
to write very much — the single most consequential
handshake of the twenty-first century.

50
00:04:03,818 --> 00:04:08,474
At the time, it was two men in a private
club, agreeing on a price.

51
00:04:11,043 --> 00:04:16,728
There was one more thing that had to be handled
before the handshake in Georgetown could

52
00:04:16,748 --> 00:04:20,618
mean anything, and it was not an
agency. It was a committee.

53
00:04:21,187 --> 00:04:26,880
In March of twenty thirty-one, the Senate Health
Committee opened hearings into the pricing

54
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:32,404
and supply of Aeterna. It was not a hostile
committee. Nine of its seventeen members had

55
00:04:32,424 --> 00:04:37,928
constituents who credited the drug with a parent's
mobility. But the chair was a senator

56
00:04:37,948 --> 00:04:43,452
from a state with no Umbrella facilities, who had
spent four years being told by her own

57
00:04:43,472 --> 00:04:48,914
hospitals that they could not afford the one
medicine everybody wanted, and she was not

58
00:04:48,934 --> 00:04:50,671
interested in being charmed.

59
00:04:51,242 --> 00:04:57,064
Umbrella sent Catherine Adeyemi, not the Director.
That was deliberate and it was her

60
00:04:57,084 --> 00:05:02,495
idea, and it was the single most effective
decision the company made that year.

61
00:05:03,185 --> 00:05:07,852
Senator, I can give you the cost per dose, the
margin, the reinvestment rate, and the

62
00:05:07,872 --> 00:05:12,594
full audited breakdown of every dollar. I brought
them. They're in the binder in front

63
00:05:12,614 --> 00:05:15,516
of you, and I'll take any question
on any line of it.

64
00:05:16,036 --> 00:05:21,619
Miss Adeyemi, what I want to know is why a hospital
in my state pays four times what a

65
00:05:21,639 --> 00:05:23,312
hospital in Delaware pays.

66
00:05:23,834 --> 00:05:28,463
Because your state's hospitals buy through three
intermediaries, Senator, and we have

67
00:05:28,483 --> 00:05:33,440
offered to sell to them directly at the Delaware price
every year since twenty twenty-nine,

68
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:38,144
and they have declined every year, because their
group purchasing contracts forbid it.

69
00:05:38,164 --> 00:05:40,278
The correspondence is in appendix four.

70
00:05:42,297 --> 00:05:47,883
It was true. Every word of it was true, and
checkable, and had been assembled over four

71
00:05:47,903 --> 00:05:53,489
months by a woman who had built her career on the
proposition that the most devastating

72
00:05:53,509 --> 00:05:58,000
thing you can do to a hostile room is to be
completely honest with it.

73
00:05:58,569 --> 00:06:04,120
The hearing lasted nine hours. It produced a
recommendation that Congress examine the

74
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:10,150
role of pharmaceutical intermediaries, which was a real
and serious problem that had nothing

75
00:06:10,170 --> 00:06:16,115
whatever to do with Umbrella, and which occupied the
committee for the following two years.

76
00:06:18,805 --> 00:06:20,172
She defended you.

77
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:23,315
She defended the accounts. They
happen to be clean.

78
00:06:23,836 --> 00:06:29,394
You have built a thing whose books are honest
and whose basement is not.

79
00:06:29,913 --> 00:06:35,520
The books being honest is what makes the basement
possible. Nobody digs under a building

80
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:38,526
that has never once lied about
its foundations.

81
00:06:42,175 --> 00:06:47,084
Assessment. Investigation turned.
Asset acquired.

82
00:06:52,466 --> 00:06:53,743
Level forty.

83
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:57,917
Forty.

84
00:06:58,487 --> 00:07:04,030
He stopped in the middle of the empty street under
the old gas lamps of Georgetown, with

85
00:07:04,050 --> 00:07:09,593
the panels hanging in the air around him like stained
glass, and felt it go through him.

86
00:07:10,163 --> 00:07:16,215
It was not like doors this time. It was not like a
floor dropping away. It was like a

87
00:07:16,235 --> 00:07:22,573
great pressure, very deep, rising — the way a
submarine must feel when it blows its tanks

88
00:07:22,593 --> 00:07:27,146
and begins, all at once, to rise toward a
surface it cannot see.

89
00:07:28,464 --> 00:07:29,536
Status.

90
00:07:34,704 --> 00:07:36,329
Charisma fifty-five.

91
00:07:36,899 --> 00:07:42,942
There is no human scale for that number. Twenty is
the limit of what the human voice has

92
00:07:42,962 --> 00:07:49,074
ever done. Thirty was more than any prophet or tyrant
in recorded history. At fifty-five,

93
00:07:49,094 --> 00:07:55,481
the Director would find over the following months that
he could walk into a room of strangers

94
00:07:55,501 --> 00:08:01,406
and, without saying a word, change what they wanted
— not all of them, not completely,

95
00:08:01,426 --> 00:08:07,332
but enough. Enough to tilt a vote. Enough to end an
argument. Enough to make a man who

96
00:08:07,352 --> 00:08:10,156
had come to accuse him leave apologising.

97
00:08:10,726 --> 00:08:16,311
The Adolf half of the mind looked at that number,
standing in the dark street, and did

98
00:08:16,331 --> 00:08:21,916
not say anything at all. It did not need to. It had
spent its whole first life wanting

99
00:08:21,936 --> 00:08:27,456
exactly this, and had had to build it from scratch,
from beer halls and banners and a

100
00:08:27,476 --> 00:08:33,648
decade of screaming. Now it had simply been handed it,
by a machine, on a street in Georgetown.

101
00:08:36,467 --> 00:08:39,177
New ability. Viral dominion.

102
00:08:44,373 --> 00:08:45,815
Purchase confirmed.

103
00:08:48,587 --> 00:08:54,291
He bought it on the street. He felt it arrive — a
new sense, like a sound at the edge

104
00:08:54,311 --> 00:09:00,284
of hearing, or a smell at the edge of memory. And two
hundred miles north, in a cold room

105
00:09:00,304 --> 00:09:06,143
at the end of a white corridor beneath the Christina
River, a grey giant in a cryogenic

106
00:09:06,163 --> 00:09:09,846
cradle turned its head, in its
sleep, toward the south.

107
00:09:12,418 --> 00:09:16,186
The first contract was real. Sloane
had kept his word.

108
00:09:16,755 --> 00:09:22,601
In September, the Director was given a security
clearance that most cabinet secretaries

109
00:09:22,621 --> 00:09:28,467
do not hold, and was driven, in a car with no
markings, through the gates of a military

110
00:09:28,487 --> 00:09:34,468
installation in Frederick, Maryland, where the United
States government had been studying

111
00:09:34,488 --> 00:09:37,501
the diseases it most feared for
eighty years.

112
00:09:38,072 --> 00:09:44,351
He was shown everything. Every programme. Every
laboratory. Every sample in every freezer.

113
00:09:44,371 --> 00:09:50,650
Every study the government had ever run on what might
happen to America if the worst thing

114
00:09:50,670 --> 00:09:54,149
in the world got loose. Every
weakness. Every gap.

115
00:09:54,719 --> 00:10:00,890
They wanted him to fix the gaps. That was the
contract. An Umbrella rapid-response vaccine

116
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:06,807
platform, for the Department of Defense — something
that could take a new pathogen and

117
00:10:06,827 --> 00:10:11,553
produce a countermeasure in seven days,
instead of twenty-two months.

118
00:10:12,124 --> 00:10:13,586
He promised them nine.

119
00:10:14,157 --> 00:10:20,158
And as he walked back out through the gates that
evening, in the unmarked car, with the

120
00:10:20,178 --> 00:10:26,525
Memory Palace quietly filing every page he had read
into a new corridor beneath the basement

121
00:10:26,545 --> 00:10:31,854
of an imaginary brick building, the Adolf half
of the mind said, very softly:

122
00:10:32,544 --> 00:10:35,197
They have shown you where they are weak.

123
00:10:35,717 --> 00:10:36,357
Yes.

124
00:10:36,879 --> 00:10:41,488
Every lock in the house. And they have given
you the key to every one.

125
00:10:42,008 --> 00:10:45,185
They've asked me to change the locks.
It's a different thing.

126
00:10:45,705 --> 00:10:46,730
Is it?

127
00:10:49,250 --> 00:10:55,589
The contract was classified. But the fact of it was
not — it appeared, as all Defense

128
00:10:55,609 --> 00:11:01,948
Department contracts of a certain size must, as a
single line in a public procurement

129
00:11:01,968 --> 00:11:06,586
database. A contract number. An agency.
An amount. And a name.

130
00:11:07,158 --> 00:11:12,868
Hannah Price found it in October, because she had
set an alert for that name five years

131
00:11:12,888 --> 00:11:18,599
before and never turned it off. She read it, and sat
very still, and then picked up her

132
00:11:18,619 --> 00:11:24,659
phone and called Owen Carrick, who now worked nights as
a security consultant for a hospital

133
00:11:24,679 --> 00:11:27,426
in Chester and answered on the first ring.

134
00:11:28,115 --> 00:11:32,197
He's inside, Owen. He's not being
investigated anymore. He's being paid.

135
00:11:32,716 --> 00:11:33,509
By who?

136
00:11:34,028 --> 00:11:37,647
By us. By the government. By the people who
were supposed to stop him.

137
00:11:39,668 --> 00:11:45,981
Carrick did not answer for a long time. When he did, his
voice was very calm — the particular,

138
00:11:46,001 --> 00:11:51,370
flat calm of a man who has already lost
everything that could be taken from him.

139
00:11:52,059 --> 00:11:55,189
Then we're going to have to find
somebody else to stop him.

140
00:11:55,710 --> 00:11:56,493
Who?

141
00:11:57,013 --> 00:11:59,451
I don't know yet. Somebody who isn't us.

142
00:12:01,971 --> 00:12:04,332
So ended the war with the agencies.

143
00:12:04,903 --> 00:12:11,341
It had lasted two years. It had cost the lives of
Howard Lessing and sixteen men with

144
00:12:11,361 --> 00:12:18,559
rifles, the career of an FBI agent, the career of a CDC
officer, a great deal of a journalist's

145
00:12:18,579 --> 00:12:25,093
peace of mind, and some of a CFO's. It had cost the
Director his deniability, once, in

146
00:12:25,113 --> 00:12:31,931
a parking lot, on a dashcam. And it had ended — not
with a trial, or a raid, or a headline

147
00:12:31,951 --> 00:12:38,618
— but with a handshake by a fire in Georgetown, and a
clearance, and a key to every lock

148
00:12:38,638 --> 00:12:39,606
in the house.

149
00:12:40,176 --> 00:12:46,636
The agencies had been beaten the way a chess player
wins. Patiently. Precisely. Each piece

150
00:12:46,656 --> 00:12:47,932
taken in its turn.

151
00:12:49,501 --> 00:12:52,759
Later, they would be beaten the way
a disaster wins.
